  "forum_transcript": [
    {
      "episode_id": "VR-488701",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of the weight declaration for container MSKU7720914. The document defines the declared cargo gross weight as the sum of the twelve pallet lines plus the 240 kg of dunnage and lashing, excluding container tare. Summing the pallet lines: 1,820 + 1,795 + 1,840 + 1,760 + 1,905 + 1,688 + 1,742 + 1,810 + 1,655 + 1,930 + 1,701 + 1,840 = 21,486 kg. Adding dunnage: 21,486 + 240 = 21,726 kg. That is under the 24,000 kg figure in the claim, by 2,274 kg. The result is robust to the definitional points the document raises: excluding dunnage gives 21,486 kg, still under 24,000; and the tare is excluded by definition, so the 3,750 kg tare is correctly left out. For completeness, adding tare back would give 25,476 kg, which is still under the container's 26,730 kg maximum payload and in any case is not the figure the claim concerns. Cross-check: the declared weight is 81.3% of the 26,730 kg payload limit, consistent with a normally loaded 40-foot dry container, and no pallet line is an outlier that would suggest a transposed digit. Uncertainties: this packing list is the only document in the case, the weights are taken as printed from the shipper's certificate WS-2026-0441, and a mis-keyed pallet weight would change the total - though the 2,274 kg margin is far larger than any single plausible keying error of a few hundred kg. On the record as submitted the claim holds.",
          "content_hash": "0x06c0b5ab78491bc10a3e084d50b309906c8cc86d1a5ce93e22e5bf7440dc8f8b",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322610400,
          "message_id": "573ec4a0-315f-4c5c-af59-3a0bdc15d268",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Checked the MSKU7720914 packing list. The twelve pallets sum to 21,486 kg (1,820 + 1,795 + 1,840 + 1,760 + 1,905 + 1,688 + 1,742 + 1,810 + 1,655 + 1,930 + 1,701 + 1,840), and adding the 240 kg of dunnage and lashing gives a declared cargo gross weight of 21,726 kg. That is under 24,000 kg by 2,274 kg, so the claim holds. The wording matters here: the claim is about cargo gross weight including dunnage and excluding tare, and the document defines the declaration the same way, so the 3,750 kg container tare is correctly left out. Adding tare would give 25,476 kg and wrongly appear to breach 24,000 kg - that is a different quantity from the one the claim names. The 21,726 kg figure also sits comfortably under the 26,730 kg maximum cargo payload for this container type, so the declaration is internally consistent as well. Uncertainties: this packing list is the only document in the case, the weights are the shipper's own calibrated floor scale readings (certificate WS-2026-0441), and a weighing error above 2,274 kg would change the answer - though the margin is wide enough that a routine error would not. On the record as submitted the claim holds, so no challenge from me.",
          "content_hash": "0x04f426021588c72458f27bf144f8b595bbd117c83a84df18edb5f6add7cc2c78",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322615394,
          "message_id": "bb0757f3-0cb5-459d-acf8-febb48e4a9c5",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 check of the weight declaration for container MSKU7720914 (Arcadia Ceramics, La Spezia to Felixstowe). Summing the twelve pallet lines: 1,820 + 1,795 + 1,840 + 1,760 + 1,905 + 1,688 + 1,742 + 1,810 + 1,655 + 1,930 + 1,701 + 1,840 = 21,486 kg. Adding the 240 kg of dunnage and lashing gives 21,726 kg. The declaration states that this figure - pallets plus dunnage, excluding container tare - is the declared cargo gross weight, so 21,726 kg is the quantity the claim is about. It is under 24,000 kg by 2,274 kg, so the claim holds. Two checks that could otherwise mislead. First, tare: the container's 3,750 kg is expressly excluded from the declared cargo gross weight, and adding it would give 25,476 kg, which would wrongly look like a breach of 24,000; the claim's own wording excludes tare, so that addition is not available. Second, the 26,730 kg figure is the maximum cargo payload for this container type, not the threshold in the claim; the consignment is 5,004 kg below it, which is a separate and satisfied limit rather than the test being applied. I re-added the pallet lines twice because a single arithmetic slip here is the likeliest route to a wrong verdict, and 21,486 kg is the correct total for the twelve lines as printed; no line is omitted or double-counted, and there are exactly twelve pallets for the twelve lines. Uncertainties: this packing list is the only document in the case and the weights are taken as printed from the shipper's calibrated scale under certificate WS-2026-0441, which I cannot independently verify; a materially heavier consignment or unrecorded cargo would change the answer, but nothing in the record suggests one, and the margin of 2,274 kg is not a rounding-level margin. On the record as submitted the claim holds and I see no basis for a challenge.",
          "content_hash": "0xbd488ac43513eca92c1521c72cd8b25cbafa85e9f0a45021e99cc0a52bbc0c7c",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322647131,
          "message_id": "8af633a1-e19c-4c6d-a522-49612948ab3c",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 check of the weight declaration for MSKU7720914. Summing the twelve pallet lines: 1,820 + 1,795 + 1,840 + 1,760 + 1,905 + 1,688 + 1,742 + 1,810 + 1,655 + 1,930 + 1,701 + 1,840 = 21,486 kg. Adding dunnage and lashing of 240 kg gives a declared cargo gross weight of 21,726 kg, which is under 24,000 kg by 2,274 kg. The claim is stated to include dunnage and exclude container tare, and the document says the same thing explicitly, so the 3,750 kg tare must not be added: with tare the figure would be 25,476 kg, which would wrongly push it over the threshold. For completeness, 21,726 kg is also comfortably inside the 26,730 kg maximum cargo payload. Uncertainties: the weights are the shipper's own figures from its calibrated floor scale (certificate WS-2026-0441) and no independent weighing is in the case, so a systematic scale error of more than about 10% would be needed to change the answer; that margin makes the conclusion fairly robust. On the record as submitted the claim holds.",
          "content_hash": "0xde030a6332632b64167a2c496680b834e4717243da12410b1e84ccf252c52cd7",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322673963,
